[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: [1mhow long should the identity survive?[0m You said "persist," which rules out in-memory, but there are two
  meaningfully different persistences and they behave differently on a shared machine.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close, browser quit, and reboot. The user stays "logged in" indefinitely until something
    explicitly clears it. This is what most people mean by "stay logged in." On a shared or public machine, the next
    person inherits the identity until logout runs.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and in-tab navigation, but dies when the tab closes. Safer by default, no stale
    identity. Costs: a second tab is a [3mseparate[0m session with no identity, which usually surprises people, and closing
    the tab by accident loses it.

  Cheap to change later — both are the same API surface behind the session module, so this is a one-line swap if you
  change your mind. I'm asking because it changes the observable behavior, not because it locks anything in.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m, since "other forms will need it later" implies forms on other pages and in other tabs
  should see the same user, and [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m would leave them blank. Either way the design includes an explicit
  [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m so logout actually works — right now there is no logout anywhere in the app, which is a gap I'll call
  out in the design rather than silently build.
